course: dictionary
lesson: 9328
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is right. The word spike can mean a sharp point, a sudden rise, or a shoe with metal points. «A rabbit's teeth can grow into sharp spikes that cut the mouth.» Here the clues are sharp and cut, so a spike is a thin, sharp point.
explain: Spike can also be a verb, an action word. To spike means to rise suddenly, or to push a sharp point into something. When you meet a word you know, ask: does this meaning fit the sentence? Then try it in the sentence to check.
explain: Think about the words around the word. Sharp points, teeth and cutting all fit a spike as a point. A graph with a sudden peak fits a spike as a sudden rise. Athletes running on a track need grip, so spikes there are shoes with short metal points on the bottom.
explain: Spike can also be a name, and a name starts with a capital letter. In «Real dogs can be very protective of their puppies and home, but among pet dogs the mother does most of the puppy care, not the father as Spike does», Spike is a dog's name, not a sharp point. Capital letters and clues tell you which spike you mean.
fact: A spike is a thin, sharp point, like on a fence, a cactus or a hedgehog.
fact: A spike can also be a sudden, sharp rise, such as «a spike in temperature».
fact: As a verb, to spike means to rise suddenly, or to stick a sharp point into something.
fact: Spikes are also running shoes with short metal points on the bottom, which give athletes grip on the track.
fact: To spike a drink means to secretly put alcohol or drugs in it. This is dangerous and against the law, so tell a grown-up straight away if it ever happens.
exercise: «A rabbit's teeth can grow into sharp spikes that cut the mouth.» What does spike mean here? | *A thin, sharp point | A sudden rise | A pair of running shoes | A loud noise
exercise: Athletes wear spikes on the track. What are spikes in this sentence? | Tall fences | *Running shoes with short metal points for grip | Sharp rocks | Tiny seeds
exercise: The graph showed «a spike in temperature». What does spike mean here? | To stick something into | A running shoe | A thin, sharp point | *A sudden, sharp rise
test: «Mangrove seeds sprout while still on the tree, drop as long spikes, and can float for months.» What shape are the seeds? | Flat like a leaf | Round like a ball | *Long and thin, like a spike | Soft and fluffy
test: Which word can mean a sudden, sharp rise? | cactus | *spike | fence | hedgehog
exam: Dave says spike only ever means a sharp point. Is he right? | Yes, a spike always means a sharp point | *No, a spike can also be a sudden rise, a running shoe, or an action word such as to rise suddenly | No, a spike is always a name | Yes, and it is always a dog's name
